regulates entry into mitosis. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>342</volume>:<fpage>39</fpage>&#x02013;45.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2682257</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Hershko, A., and A. Ciechanover. <year>1998</year>. The ubiquitin system. <source>Annu. Rev. Biochem.</source> <volume>67</volume>:<fpage>425</fpage>&#x02013;479.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9759494</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Huang, H., C.A. Joazeiro, E. Bonfoco, S. Kamada, J.D. Leverson, and T. Hunter. <year>2000</year>. The inhibitor of apoptosis, cIAP2, functions as a ubiquitin-protein ligase and promotes in vitro monoubiquitination of caspases 3 and 7. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>275</volume>:<fpage>26661</fpage>&#x02013;26664.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10862606</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Izumi, T., and J.L. Maller. <year>1993</year>. Elimination of cdc2 phosphorylation sites in the cdc25 phosphatase blocks initiation of M-phase. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>4</volume>:<fpage>1337</fpage>&#x02013;1350.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7513216</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Izumi, T., D.H. Walker, and J.L. Maller. <year>1992</year>. Periodic changes in phosphorylation of the <italic>Xenopus</italic> cdc25 phosphatase regulate its activity. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>3</volume>:<fpage>927</fpage>&#x02013;939.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1392080</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Jackson, P.K., A.G. Eldridge, E. Freed, L. Furstenthal, J.Y. Hsu, B.K. Kaiser, and J.D. Reimann. <year>2000</year>. The lore of the RINGs: substrate recognition and catalysis by ubiquitin ligases. <source>Trends Cell Biol.</source> <volume>10</volume>:<fpage>429</fpage>&#x02013;439.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10998601</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Jentsch, S., W. Seufert, T. Sommer, and H.A. Reins. <year>1990</year>. Ubiquitin-conjugating enzymes: novel regulators of eukaryotic cells. <source>Trends Biochem. Sci.</source> <volume>15</volume>:<fpage>195</fpage>&#x02013;198.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2193438</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Jha, M.N., J.R. Bamburg, and J.S. Bedford. <year>1994</year>. Cell cycle arrest by Colcemid differs in human normal and tumor cells. <source>Cancer Res.</source> <volume>54</volume>:<fpage>5011</fpage>&#x02013;5015.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7915197</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Joazeiro, C.A., and A.M. Weissman. <year>2000</year>. RING finger proteins: mediators of ubiquitin ligase activity. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>102</volume>:<fpage>549</fpage>&#x02013;552.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11007473</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Joazeiro, C.A., S.S. Wing, H. Huang, J.D. Leverson, T. Hunter, and Y.C. Liu. <year>1999</year>. The tyrosine kinase negative regulator c-Cbl as a RING-type, E2-dependent ubiquitin-protein ligase. <source>Science.</source> <volume>286</volume>:<fpage>309</fpage>&#x02013;312.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10514377</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">King, R.W., P.K. Jackson, and M.W. Kirschner. <year>1994</year>. Mitosis in transition. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>79</volume>:<fpage>563</fpage>&#x02013;571.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7954823</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">King, R.W., R.J. Deshaies, J.M. Peters, and M.W. Kirschner. <year>1996</year>. How proteolysis drives the cell cycle. <source>Science.</source> <volume>274</volume>:<fpage>1652</fpage>&#x02013;1659.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8939846</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Kornbluth, S., B. Sebastian, T. Hunter, and J. Newport. <year>1994</year>. Membrane localization of the kinase which phosphorylates p34cdc2 on threonine 14. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>5</volume>:<fpage>273</fpage>&#x02013;282.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8049520</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Krek, W. <year>1998</year>. Proteolysis and the G1-S transition: the SCF connection. <source>Curr. Opin. Genet. Dev.</source> <volume>8</volume>:<fpage>36</fpage>&#x02013;42.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9529603</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Kumagai, A., and W.G. Dunphy. <year>1991</year>. The cdc25 protein controls tyrosine dephosphorylation of the cdc2 protein in a cell-free system. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>64</volume>:<fpage>903</fpage>&#x02013;914.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1825803</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Kumagai, A., and W.G. Dunphy. <year>1992</year>. Regulation of the cdc25 protein during the cell cycle in <italic>Xenopus</italic> extracts. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>70</volume>:<fpage>139</fpage>&#x02013;151.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1623517</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Kumagai, A., and W.G. Dunphy. <year>1996</year>. Purification and molecular cloning of Plx1, a Cdc25-regulatory kinase from <italic>Xenopus</italic> egg extracts. <source>Science.</source> <volume>273</volume>:<fpage>1377</fpage>&#x02013;1380.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8703070</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Lane, H.A., and E.A. Nigg. <year>1996</year>. Antibody microinjection reveals an essential role for human polo-like kinase 1 (Plk1) in the functional maturation of mitotic centrosomes. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>135</volume>:<fpage>1701</fpage>&#x02013;1713.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8991084</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Leverson, J.D., C.A. Joazeir